FILED s532a (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-01), found probing #4085.  PRE-EXISTING (base 10cdc985 identical).  LOUD (a run-time die).

    my $r = ""; open(Foo::OUT, ">", \$r) or die;
    eval "package Foo; print {OUT} qq{c}; 1" or print "ERR $@";
    close Foo::OUT; print "[$r]\n";
  perl 5.40.3: [c]
  PCL: ERR Undefined subroutine &Foo::OUT called at (eval 1) line 1.  / []

The file-level spelling works when the file opens OUT itself (`open(OUT, ...); print {OUT} "c"`, and `open(Foo::OUT,...); package Foo; print {OUT} "c"`): the emitter knows OUT is a filehandle there.  In the eval unit OUT is never seen as a handle, so the block's bareword is classified as a CALL ((p-list-ctx (Foo::pl-OUT))).  perl (no strict subs): a bareword that is not a known sub, alone in a print BLOCK, is the handle name.  Layer: Pl/ (the print-block filehandle classifier).  Probe: scratch/s532a/p/ev1.pl in the s532a worktree.
